## RT-7. Temp DLL (investigated 2026-10-20, read-only on the product)
- The restore built `%TEMP%\live_stereo_perroomk_restore.dll` and copied it to `live_stereo.dll` (both `970e63bb08e5092875b49796ce01ab7ce24dbb01`, 285184). The DLL embeds its own file name (export module name and the PDB name `live_stereo_perroomk_restore.pdb`), and the name changes `.rdata`, `.buildid` and the RIP-relative offsets in `.text`.
- Proof (scratch dir `%TEMP%\dllprobe\`, recipe `software\.venv\Scripts\python.exe -m ziglang cc -shared -O3 -o <out> software/engine/live_stereo.c`, zig 0.16.0): building as `live_stereo_perroomk_restore.dll` gives `.text` / `.rdata` / `.pdata` byte-identical to the Temp DLL (only `.buildid` differs, it holds the path). Building as `live_stereo.dll` twice at one path gives the same sha1 both times (`fbd41bb1...`): **zig is deterministic for the same output path**. -O2, -g and -march=native give the same `.text` as -O3; -Os, -ffp-contract=off and explicit gnu/msvc targets differ, so the recipe is plain `-shared -O3`.
- **Verdict: benign.** Same C (`bbdf5799`, which PERROOMK did not change), same flags, different output file name. No code difference.
- **Step 1 Phase 0 (before any edit):** copy the current Temp DLL and `live_stereo.pdb` to `%TEMP%\live_stereo_970e_keep.*`, then rebuild with the recipe straight to `$env:LOCALAPPDATA\Temp\live_stereo.dll`. Expect `dcde61df...` (the pin). If it matches, the pin is unchanged. If not, pin the new hash, report it, and run the DLL-based quick tests (`test_bloom_off`, `test_quick_pins`) to prove behaviour. Not a STOP either way.
- Every later DLL build in this run and in any restore goes straight to `live_stereo.dll` (never build under another name and copy).

